1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:11,420
For hundreds of years before Christ, and hundreds more after, Europe's indigenous tribes did battle.

2
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:19,740
Their world was harsh and savage, in which survival was won by those with the deadliest weapons and the will to use them.

3
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:29,740
Their tribal names exude a certain swagger to this very day.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
The Huns, Vandals, Goths, Saxons, Celts and Franks.

5
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:46,360
The Greeks and Romans branded them barbarians,

6
00:00:46,361 --> 00:00:50,240
considering them uncivilized, vile, more animal than human.

7
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:56,480
The term barbarian originally comes from the Greek.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,960
And it's what the Greeks called everybody who wasn't a Greek speaker.

9
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:09,940
The Romans take it up and they refer it to everybody that they considered to be non-Roman outside the empire.

10
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:13,800
People themselves certainly didn't call themselves barbarians.

11
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:21,100
The so-called barbarians lived during the Iron Age, an era of war and emerging technologies,

12
00:01:21,740 --> 00:01:24,700
when metalsmiths forged the weapons that shaped Europe.

13
00:01:24,701 --> 00:01:34,740
No matter when man has fought, there have been technological devices that he's used to increase his ability to fight.

14
00:01:35,220 --> 00:01:38,740
A club is better than the hands. A sword is better than a club.

15
00:01:38,741 --> 00:01:43,860
And in the case of the barbarians, we have actually very strong technology.

16
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:53,720
The British Museum in London plays host to barbarian weapons once wielded in the fury of combat.

17
00:01:54,700 --> 00:02:04,360
Among them is the 2,300-year-old Kirkburn sword.

18
00:02:07,140 --> 00:02:12,080
The sword was unearthed in 1987 from a gravesite in Kirkburn, Yorkshire.

19
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:16,360
Its hilt was made of bronze, animal horn, and red glass.

20
00:02:17,260 --> 00:02:23,100
Its blade, nearly three feet long, was forged from the metal that changed the face of ancient combat, iron.

21
00:02:23,101 --> 00:02:31,080
It was crafted, and no doubt used, with lethal efficiency, by one of history's most feared barbarian tribes.

22
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:35,960
The Celts.

23
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:43,840
In 800 BC, the Celts dominated much of Europe.

24
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:47,320
By the first century, they had settled in Britain.

25
00:02:47,321 --> 00:02:54,320
The Celts are a very obscure people because they date from pre-Roman times when we don't

26
00:02:54,321 --> 00:02:58,320
have historical records, so essentially we know about them from archaeology.

27
00:02:58,321 --> 00:03:07,320
Archaeologists have discovered Celtic artifacts, showcasing the mastery of the culture's weapons makers.

28
00:03:07,321 --> 00:03:18,320
Hector Cole is a world-renowned ironsmith, skilled in the traditional methods used by

29
00:03:18,321 --> 00:03:20,298
Celtic sword makers.

30
00:03:20,299 --> 00:03:27,320
Like his brethren before him, he relies on instinct as much as science.

31
00:03:27,321 --> 00:03:34,018
When you put your metal in the fire, your mind goes in the fire with it.

32
00:03:34,019 --> 00:03:35,320
If it doesn't,

33
00:03:35,321 --> 00:03:37,380
then you will lose control of it.

34
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:39,780
And a couple of degrees too high,

35
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:42,300
you will burn the metal and it's no good.

36
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:44,140
A few degrees below,

37
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:46,860
and the pieces of metal will not hammer together

38
00:03:46,861 --> 00:03:47,700
and become one.

39
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:52,400
The earliest Celt smiths forged weapons from bronze.

40
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:55,620
By the 5th century BC,

41
00:03:55,621 --> 00:03:58,840
they were fashioning harder, stronger, deadlier arms,

42
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:01,780
like the Kirkburn sword from iron.

43
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:09,840
Carefully working fire and bellows, Hector has heated a chunk of iron ore,

44
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:12,560
extracting the iron into a catch basin below.

45
00:04:13,780 --> 00:04:17,020
He then molded the iron to form three sword-length billets.

46
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:20,740
Hector now begins to fuse the billets together to make a blade.

47
00:04:25,620 --> 00:04:33,140
I've taken the rocks from the ground and heated them and made them into iron.

48
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:35,140
That in itself is magic.

49
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:41,200
So you can see how these swords built up a reputation through their mysticism.

50
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:47,040
And if it was a good sword, a good sword was prized above anything else.

51
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:52,320
So carrying on, you can see now it's starting to take shape.

52
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:58,240
The charcoal in which the iron billets heat up contains traces of carbon.

53
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:04,160
The carbon acts as a hardening agent, and by pounding it into the iron, Hector produces steel.

54
00:05:04,161 --> 00:05:09,160
up to a limit. The higher the carbon content, the stronger the steel.

55
00:05:09,161 --> 00:05:16,160
After the shaft cools, Hector hammers its sides, compacting the metal. The result is

56
00:05:16,161 --> 00:05:23,160
the kind of hard, powerful steel edge that bloodied and killed countless Celtic foes.

57
00:05:23,161 --> 00:05:32,036
The Celtic sword was, on the whole, a slashing weapon so that you were cutting.

58
00:05:32,037 --> 00:05:33,160
You didn't

59
00:05:33,161 --> 00:05:36,760
You didn't want to be sticking it into your opponent.

60
00:05:36,761 --> 00:05:40,560
You were battering them down, battering their shield down,

61
00:05:40,561 --> 00:05:43,760
and then slicing into them to immobilize them.

62
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:54,760
Finally, Hector forges a tongue where an ornamental handle

63
00:05:54,761 --> 00:05:59,760
will slide atop the cold hard steel of the Celts' most prized killing tool.

64
00:05:59,761 --> 00:06:03,800
The Celtic sword was about 30 inches long.

65
00:06:04,940 --> 00:06:08,500
A rival weapon, the Roman Gladius, was similar in length,

66
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:13,240
but had a sharp-edged blade with a short-pointed tip designed for stabbing.

67
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:18,120
The Celtic sword featured a heavier blade, more effective for slashing.

68
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:24,540
60 hours of hard hammering, forging away, grinding, polishing,

69
00:06:24,541 --> 00:06:26,880
and getting a handle put on it,

70
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,240
we now have a Celtic sword

71
00:06:30,241 --> 00:06:35,120
that is fit to give to any warrior to take into battle.

72
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:44,560
The Celts augmented their masterful weaponry

73
00:06:44,561 --> 00:06:46,180
with a unique system of defense.

74
00:06:47,140 --> 00:06:49,380
They devised ingenious fortified dwellings,

75
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:52,060
many of which still slice into the English countryside.

76
00:06:52,061 --> 00:06:59,560
In Oswestry, England, lie the contours of a 2,600-year-old Celtic hill fort,

77
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:05,300
a far cry from the era's classic square structures with towering walls.

78
00:07:06,860 --> 00:07:11,620
Seizing the high ground, the Celts used the natural topography of the land to their advantage.

79
00:07:12,580 --> 00:07:16,120
We can see that the Celts chose this spot for the views,

80
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:20,420
the fact that you can see all around of anybody coming to attack,

81
00:07:20,421 --> 00:07:25,140
and they have built a series of banks going all the way up to the top.

82
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:31,200
Oswestry was fortified around its perimeter with a series of earthen barriers.

83
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:35,180
The lower mounds were crowned with a wooden palisade.

84
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:38,240
The upper mounds were topped with stone walls.

85
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:44,000
Atop the hill was a clearing where hundreds of Celts lived in thatched roof homes.

86
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:48,960
Not only have we got these multiple banks and ditches coming up through,

87
00:07:48,961 --> 00:07:56,020
But we've also got a bank which runs along the entrance on both sides of it

88
00:07:56,021 --> 00:08:00,220
so that it cuts off anybody being able to get into the ditches,

89
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:04,460
up onto the top of the banks, and attack the hill fort in that way.

90
00:08:05,480 --> 00:08:09,840
So they have to come along that track down there.

91
00:08:10,780 --> 00:08:13,160
Invaders who dared to attack through the front entrance

92
00:08:13,161 --> 00:08:17,200
soon became trapped in a two-sided free-fire killing zone.

93
00:08:17,201 --> 00:08:22,280
Those fortunate enough to battle their way through faced a fortified gate at the top.

94
00:08:23,020 --> 00:08:25,400
On each side of the gate waited yet another obstacle.

95
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:33,220
If you look at the bank in front of us here, it is still very steep, quite high, very steep.

96
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:36,160
If you're attacking, then it's a considerable slope.

97
00:08:36,620 --> 00:08:39,080
You've got the stone wall on the top, possibly timber,

98
00:08:39,081 --> 00:08:42,640
but also you're going to have people up there throwing things at you,

99
00:08:42,641 --> 00:08:47,640
and that really does make it almost impossible to get up to the top.

100
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:53,780
Yet despite the several layers of protection, Celtic hill forts were not impenetrable.

101
00:08:56,640 --> 00:09:01,900
In the early 1st century, the Roman army descended on the Celts and Britain.

102
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:09,120
In 43 AD, the Romans invade Britain under Claudius, the Emperor Claudius,

103
00:09:09,121 --> 00:09:12,540
and they face substantial opposition,

104
00:09:13,480 --> 00:09:17,480
but disunited opposition

105
00:09:17,481 --> 00:09:21,320
because the Celtic tribes were not a single unit.

106
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:26,060
The Romans prevailed and occupied the island.

107
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:29,120
After bristling for 20 years under Roman rule,

108
00:09:29,580 --> 00:09:33,300
Celtic warrior Queen Boudicca led her people on a bloody revolt.

109
00:09:39,120 --> 00:09:47,500
Spear in hand, Boudicca stormed into battle riding one of the finest examples of barbarian

110
00:09:47,501 --> 00:09:50,020
technology, the Celtic chariot.

111
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:55,880
The Celtic chariot becomes a very important part of their myth and their legends.

112
00:09:56,680 --> 00:10:03,420
These became very important symbols, not just war weapons, but symbols of greatness, of

113
00:10:03,421 --> 00:10:04,300
warrior greatness.

114
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:07,820
Here, I think we've got the lower jaw.

115
00:10:07,821 --> 00:10:13,380
In 2001, archaeologists unearthed a grave containing remnants of a Celtic chariot

116
00:10:13,381 --> 00:10:16,240
that would answer a puzzling riddle about its technology.

117
00:10:17,240 --> 00:10:21,240
Experts had never been able to figure out how the chariot's wheels were secured to the axle.

118
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:27,760
At the site, ground X-ray revealed a crucial clue, a J-shaped pin.

119
00:10:29,640 --> 00:10:31,700
And this is the drawing that really...

120
00:10:31,701 --> 00:10:32,401
Yes.

121
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:35,140
The pin intrigued Mike Lodes and Robert Hereford,

122
00:10:35,141 --> 00:10:39,200
who were trying to construct an accurate, fully functional Celtic chariot.

123
00:10:40,180 --> 00:10:44,500
Their only chance to succeed was to solve the mystery of the pin's role.

124
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:49,360
They were these J-shaped linchpins, quite an interesting shape.

125
00:10:50,260 --> 00:10:54,820
And associated with them in the grave was a little iron ring.

126
00:10:55,880 --> 00:11:01,040
Now, we puzzled for ages how this might have worked.

127
00:11:01,041 --> 00:11:07,540
The solution came when research revealed that Celtic wheelmakers cut a groove in their chariot axles.

128
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:11,940
So this J shape actually fits in the slot.

129
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:17,940
Then this comes around that and ties to the ring.

130
00:11:18,620 --> 00:11:20,720
So keeping it in place.

131
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:25,820
The next step in construction was to form the charioteer's platform.

132
00:11:25,821 --> 00:11:38,640
The platform is a latticework, all made from a single strip of rawhide, and that gives tremendous spring.

133
00:11:39,380 --> 00:11:44,220
Most chariots in the world, you stand up on them, whether it's an Egyptian chariot, an Assyrian chariot,

134
00:11:44,221 --> 00:11:50,000
a Roman racing chariot or a Greek chariot, you stand, because not only have you got some suspension

135
00:11:50,001 --> 00:11:52,540
in the plaited rawhide platform,

136
00:11:52,760 --> 00:11:54,740
but your legs are springs.

137
00:11:54,860 --> 00:11:57,640
So you're very much like a surfer riding away.

138
00:11:58,320 --> 00:12:00,640
A Celtic chariot was built for two,

139
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:02,560
an armed warrior who stood

140
00:12:02,561 --> 00:12:04,780
and a driver who sat.

141
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:07,820
By itself, the platform's springy latticework

142
00:12:07,821 --> 00:12:10,140
wouldn't prevent either from being jostled

143
00:12:10,141 --> 00:12:11,340
by every bump and rut.

144
00:12:11,980 --> 00:12:13,920
But Lodes believes the Celts incorporated

145
00:12:13,921 --> 00:12:16,800
another design element to ensure a smoother ride.

146
00:12:16,801 --> 00:12:20,880
What if these Ys were straps?

147
00:12:20,881 --> 00:12:23,040
Rawhide straps.

148
00:12:23,041 --> 00:12:27,900
And then the platform could be slung in these straps.

149
00:12:28,360 --> 00:12:33,740
Now you've got something that's got lateral and forward movement.

150
00:12:34,160 --> 00:12:36,300
It's tied off underneath so it's not too extreme,

151
00:12:36,760 --> 00:12:41,340
but it just gives a wonderfully suspended platform,

152
00:12:42,080 --> 00:12:44,740
able to cover almost any terrain.

153
00:12:46,800 --> 00:12:51,800
The Celtic chariot suspension no doubt enhanced its performance on the battlefield,

154
00:12:51,801 --> 00:12:56,800
where it may have been used much like today's armored personnel carriers.

155
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:04,800
The charioteers would take their warriors close to the front line and drop them off.

156
00:13:04,801 --> 00:13:08,800
The warrior would get off and this is where he would come in and he would fight.

157
00:13:08,801 --> 00:13:12,740
fight.

158
00:13:12,741 --> 00:13:16,000
So after a few minutes, your charioteer

159
00:13:16,001 --> 00:13:18,220
would come racing around the line.

160
00:13:18,221 --> 00:13:22,840
And still at the gallop, you would get back onto your chariot,

161
00:13:22,841 --> 00:13:25,880
saying goodbye to the enemy, and have a break

162
00:13:25,881 --> 00:13:27,520
while another man was dropped off.

163
00:13:27,521 --> 00:13:31,480
Constant rotation of front line troops.

164
00:13:34,600 --> 00:13:36,220
And Queen Boudicca?

165
00:13:36,221 --> 00:13:38,280
We left her leading the Celts in a pivotal battle

166
00:13:38,281 --> 00:13:39,960
against the Romans in AD 60.

167
00:13:41,100 --> 00:13:43,860
She counted on her superior chariots to overwhelm them.

168
00:13:44,820 --> 00:13:46,960
But barbarian technology in this case

169
00:13:46,961 --> 00:13:49,820
proved no match for the empire's disciplined troops.

170
00:13:51,540 --> 00:13:54,700
And as they gradually made their advance,

171
00:13:55,200 --> 00:13:59,300
Boudicca's army was condensed into a smaller mass.

172
00:14:00,140 --> 00:14:02,800
And they got hemmed in and pressed in.

173
00:14:02,801 --> 00:14:04,380
The chariots couldn't maneuver.

174
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:06,080
The warriors couldn't fight.

175
00:14:06,460 --> 00:14:07,620
And it was carnage.

176
00:14:08,280 --> 00:14:11,240
and she was wiped out and her army was wiped out.

177
00:14:14,360 --> 00:14:20,740
After the Roman invasion, the Celts are the settled native population of Britain

178
00:14:20,741 --> 00:14:23,200
and they gradually become Romanized.

179
00:14:26,180 --> 00:14:29,260
The mighty Romans had secured another conquest.

180
00:14:32,260 --> 00:14:36,980
But across the English Channel, other barbarian tribes were growing restless.

181
00:14:38,280 --> 00:14:45,280
One wielding a fearsome new weapon would one day threaten to topple the empire.

182
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:58,280
By the end of the 4th century AD, Rome was disintegrating.

183
00:14:58,281 --> 00:15:03,280
Civil war and political upheaval had torn the vast empire in two.

184
00:15:03,281 --> 00:15:12,800
Meanwhile, the Germanic barbarian tribes in the north were being forced toward Rome by a new group of barbarians that had swept in from the steppes of Asia.

185
00:15:15,300 --> 00:15:20,780
They flooded into Europe on horseback, raiding and killing with swift and brutal efficiency.

186
00:15:22,200 --> 00:15:24,700
They called themselves the Huns.

187
00:15:26,860 --> 00:15:28,940
The Huns soon reached the Danube.

188
00:15:29,540 --> 00:15:32,100
On the other side of the river lay the Roman Empire.

189
00:15:32,900 --> 00:15:35,640
The arrival of the Huns was a terrible shock.

190
00:15:36,060 --> 00:15:39,420
It was a shock to the Germanic peoples, it was a shock to the Romans.

191
00:15:40,960 --> 00:15:44,440
They also came with this formidable war technology.

192
00:15:45,740 --> 00:15:51,440
The Huns came armed with an odd-looking yet devastating weapon, the recurve bow.

193
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,100
It featured horn-shaped tips and a curved shaft.

194
00:15:55,101 --> 00:15:59,660
Out on the steps, where there are very few trees,

195
00:16:00,500 --> 00:16:04,560
there's much less availability of that sort of raw material to make a bow out of.

196
00:16:05,400 --> 00:16:10,820
So they were forced, by what was available, to develop something else.

197
00:16:11,860 --> 00:16:15,080
And what they do is to take a thin lath of wood,

198
00:16:15,300 --> 00:16:18,200
instead of a chunk of tree, they take a thin lath of wood.

199
00:16:18,920 --> 00:16:23,780
This signature Hun weapon was a marked contrast to the bow most commonly used in Europe.

200
00:16:25,100 --> 00:16:32,100
The western longbow was carved from a single limb of a tree and was only as strong as the wood used to produce it.

201
00:16:32,101 --> 00:16:37,100
Its power came from its length and flexibility, allowing a deep draw.

202
00:16:37,101 --> 00:16:43,100
The shorter, recurve, Hunbow was made up of several layers of wood glued together against the grain.

203
00:16:43,101 --> 00:16:50,100
The horn-shaped tips made the bow curve back on itself, producing added torque and a quick snapping action.

204
00:16:50,101 --> 00:16:54,100
But which bow harnessed more power?

205
00:16:54,101 --> 00:17:01,600
Using a kind of sophisticated stopwatch called a chronograph, it's possible to compare the

206
00:17:01,601 --> 00:17:06,160
velocity of an arrow shot from each type of bow.

207
00:17:06,161 --> 00:17:11,340
As you can see, there's a sensor at the front here and a sensor at the rear here.

208
00:17:11,341 --> 00:17:16,780
And as the arrow passes across the chronograph, switches the timing on as it passes the front

209
00:17:16,781 --> 00:17:22,340
one, switches it off again as it passes the rear one, and so the device will then record

210
00:17:22,341 --> 00:17:24,500
the velocity in feet per second.

211
00:17:24,501 --> 00:17:27,200
And as you see, it's showing the display that it's ready.

212
00:17:27,201 --> 00:17:30,840
So we'll try the Hunbow first.

213
00:17:30,841 --> 00:17:34,940
107.4, so that's about 75 miles an hour.

214
00:17:34,941 --> 00:17:37,920
Must remember that the bows we're using here today

215
00:17:37,921 --> 00:17:40,880
are only about half as powerful as the ones they were using.

216
00:17:40,881 --> 00:17:43,340
So we could translate that up.

217
00:17:43,341 --> 00:17:46,580
The Hunbow might be reaching 100 miles an hour

218
00:17:46,581 --> 00:17:48,100
at the full power.

219
00:17:48,101 --> 00:17:50,860
So here we go with the longbow.

220
00:17:50,861 --> 00:17:55,400
99.22 feet per second. That's about 68 miles an hour.

221
00:17:55,660 --> 00:18:00,660
And we can see that we've lost a fair bit of velocity there in proportion to the power of the bow.

222
00:18:00,661 --> 00:18:06,060
It shows that the Hunbow, with its faster action, its more efficient energy storing,

223
00:18:06,180 --> 00:18:11,440
its more efficient energy releasing, is punching that arrow out significantly higher velocity.

224
00:18:12,520 --> 00:18:14,980
And that's where the difference is going to lie.

225
00:18:14,981 --> 00:18:22,180
When we get to the Hunnic bow, we're looking at a weapon that could penetrate some, if

226
00:18:22,181 --> 00:18:28,060
not much, of the armor of the individuals that were opposing them.

227
00:18:28,061 --> 00:18:35,980
Certainly the breastplates or the segmented or scale armors of the Romans could be penetrated

228
00:18:35,981 --> 00:18:40,980
because the metal was not thick, the leather underneath was not thick.

229
00:18:40,981 --> 00:18:49,460
Beyond its superior power, the Hun bow held another advantage over the longbow.

230
00:18:50,160 --> 00:18:53,360
It could be fired from horseback with astounding precision.

231
00:18:54,820 --> 00:18:59,580
Technology is not just for the killing. It's for the frightening aspect.

232
00:19:00,220 --> 00:19:07,180
And a Hun bearing down on somebody with a bolt that he could fire with great accuracy from a very strong horse.

233
00:19:07,181 --> 00:19:12,860
The man who was likely to run away was defeated as much as the man who was penetrated by the arrow.

234
00:19:15,680 --> 00:19:19,420
By AD 430, the Huns were following a new leader.

235
00:19:20,660 --> 00:19:24,260
Attila, the warrior synonymous with the term barbarian.

236
00:19:27,040 --> 00:19:32,520
He commanded thousands of battle-hardened archers, capable of striking with deadly accuracy.

237
00:19:32,521 --> 00:19:38,520
Their tactics introduced a new dimension to the battlefield.

238
00:19:38,521 --> 00:19:42,636
The advantage for the Huns was simple, hit and run tactics.

239
00:19:42,637 --> 00:19:44,520
Quite simply being able to,

240
00:19:44,521 --> 00:19:50,520
within seconds, get yourself within bow range, within arrow range, fire off a quiver full of

241
00:19:50,521 --> 00:19:57,520
arrows, 20, 30, 40 arrows even, at an enemy which cannot reach you, and then disappearing again to fill your quiver.

242
00:19:57,521 --> 00:20:02,520
The Hun bow was tailor-made for this hit-and-run style.

243
00:20:02,521 --> 00:20:08,280
Unlike the longbow, the short and powerful Hun bow is very maneuverable, allowing it

244
00:20:08,281 --> 00:20:16,960
to be used from both sides of the horse and also giving a great range of fire.

245
00:20:16,961 --> 00:20:20,840
Historians still debate whether or not Hun horsemen used stirrups to help them keep their

246
00:20:20,841 --> 00:20:21,840
balance.

247
00:20:21,841 --> 00:20:26,320
If they did, their prowess on the battlefield could have only been enhanced.

248
00:20:27,680 --> 00:20:33,440
The stirrup is vital for combat on horseback, absolutely vital.

249
00:20:34,200 --> 00:20:39,660
It allows you to distribute your weight over a much greater area on the horse's back,

250
00:20:39,860 --> 00:20:45,960
and it creates effectively a three-point fighting platform, your feet and your butt.

251
00:20:45,961 --> 00:20:52,640
and this enables you with a bow to lean right forward, over to the side and back.

252
00:21:01,520 --> 00:21:06,780
By 4.40, Attila's army had thundered through a wide swath of southern Europe.

253
00:21:06,781 --> 00:21:16,620
But as they moved west, it was slowed by an obstacle they never confronted on their native steps.

254
00:21:18,040 --> 00:21:25,420
The further into the forest and woodland they came, the less could they maneuver their horse archers at high speed.

255
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:30,120
The unaccustomed terrain wasn't the Huns' only problem.

256
00:21:30,121 --> 00:21:37,760
In 451 in Gaul, they clashed with the combined might of Roman legions and other barbarian tribes.

257
00:21:38,120 --> 00:21:40,320
The fearsome Huns fell at last.

258
00:21:42,100 --> 00:21:46,360
One of the barbarian tribes that helped defeat them was the Goths.

259
00:21:46,840 --> 00:21:51,260
They were destined to be the first barbarians to storm through the gates of Rome.

260
00:21:55,860 --> 00:21:59,600
When the marauding Huns tore through Europe in the 4th century,

261
00:22:00,120 --> 00:22:03,300
Several barbarian tribes fled in their wake.

262
00:22:04,940 --> 00:22:07,740
One of the displaced tribes was the Goths.

263
00:22:09,480 --> 00:22:12,980
Driven to the border of the Roman Empire in A.D. 376,

264
00:22:13,340 --> 00:22:16,500
the Goths had no choice but to cut a deal with imperial officials.

265
00:22:17,900 --> 00:22:20,520
In exchange for asylum in Roman territory,

266
00:22:20,521 --> 00:22:23,880
they agreed to fight for the declining and divided empire.

267
00:22:26,040 --> 00:22:29,980
Under Rome's thumb, these barbarians proved they had more to offer

268
00:22:29,981 --> 00:22:31,480
than just military manpower.

269
00:22:32,780 --> 00:22:35,080
The Goths were renowned metallurgists

270
00:22:35,081 --> 00:22:37,360
who crafted intricate and ornate jewelry.

271
00:22:38,700 --> 00:22:40,080
And the coins they made

272
00:22:40,081 --> 00:22:41,980
reflected the same masterful workmanship

273
00:22:41,981 --> 00:22:44,020
they used to produce their weaponry.

274
00:22:47,320 --> 00:22:50,500
They were particularly good at refining silver,

275
00:22:50,680 --> 00:22:53,000
so their coins had a value above

276
00:22:53,001 --> 00:22:54,940
what most people would have expected

277
00:22:54,941 --> 00:22:56,260
for silver coins at their time.

278
00:22:56,880 --> 00:22:59,320
The coins were about 90% pure silver.

279
00:22:59,980 --> 00:23:01,980
Working from a sheet of silver,

280
00:23:01,981 --> 00:23:04,980
goth coin makers began by striking round blanks

281
00:23:04,981 --> 00:23:06,980
with a hole punch.

282
00:23:06,981 --> 00:23:10,980
Then you need a pair of dyes.

283
00:23:10,981 --> 00:23:15,980
These are struck and made with tiny punches.

284
00:23:15,981 --> 00:23:19,980
And here the expression, to make money,

285
00:23:19,981 --> 00:23:22,980
hand over fist.

286
00:23:22,981 --> 00:23:27,980
And the coin is worth about $50.

287
00:23:27,981 --> 00:23:32,980
On the back of the coin is a small cross.

288
00:23:32,981 --> 00:23:39,980
The way that you get small occurrences is by the simple procedure of using the cutting guide,

289
00:23:39,981 --> 00:23:46,980
the small cross in the middle of the coin, so that you cut the coin in half, achieving two half pennies.

290
00:23:46,981 --> 00:23:52,980
While goth coins have endured for centuries, few goth weapons have survived.

291
00:23:52,981 --> 00:23:56,980
But scholars believe we can understand what the goths wielded in battle.

292
00:23:56,981 --> 00:23:58,880
by examining Roman weaponry,

293
00:23:59,280 --> 00:24:02,680
as the technology of the two cultures eventually merged.

294
00:24:03,300 --> 00:24:06,340
The auxiliary units of the Roman army included people

295
00:24:06,341 --> 00:24:08,980
who were drawn from, quotes, barbarian lands,

296
00:24:09,100 --> 00:24:11,140
and the customs that they brought with them,

297
00:24:11,460 --> 00:24:13,360
whether it was for decorating their weaponry,

298
00:24:13,400 --> 00:24:14,880
or indeed the weapons themselves.

299
00:24:15,360 --> 00:24:17,620
Well, sometimes there's not very much difference, therefore,

300
00:24:17,720 --> 00:24:19,880
between the weapons that the Romans were using

301
00:24:19,881 --> 00:24:22,320
and the weapons that barbarians were using.

302
00:24:23,020 --> 00:24:25,860
Just as Roman arms makers copied the Goths,

303
00:24:25,861 --> 00:24:27,260
the Goths copied the Romans.

304
00:24:28,520 --> 00:24:31,360
They have adopted a lot of the ways of the Roman people,

305
00:24:31,540 --> 00:24:32,340
particularly armor.

306
00:24:32,900 --> 00:24:34,460
We have on the top here a riveted helmet.

307
00:24:35,080 --> 00:24:37,800
The 4th century saw a change in the way helmets are made.

308
00:24:37,880 --> 00:24:38,780
They're often made as segments,

309
00:24:38,880 --> 00:24:40,780
which are then riveted together into a rigid armor.

310
00:24:41,140 --> 00:24:42,460
We've got the crest across the top here

311
00:24:42,461 --> 00:24:44,360
to protect against blows coming down from the side,

312
00:24:44,740 --> 00:24:47,380
and a movable neck piece at the back,

313
00:24:47,680 --> 00:24:49,480
and the nasal guard there,

314
00:24:50,120 --> 00:24:51,420
obviously protecting the nose from chops.

315
00:24:52,520 --> 00:24:54,580
Goth soldiers were clad in mail.

316
00:24:54,581 --> 00:24:59,180
the pliable metal body armor used in the Roman army before the birth of Christ.

317
00:25:00,720 --> 00:25:07,160
Mail itself is just made from rings, which are, in this case, butted together,

318
00:25:08,020 --> 00:25:14,960
forming a very flexible suit of armor, which sword-slipes and attacks and cuts just slide clean off of.

319
00:25:15,500 --> 00:25:18,440
Later on, we see an increasing amount of riveted mail,

320
00:25:18,860 --> 00:25:21,960
because the problem with this is a spear or an arrow can push the rings apart.

321
00:25:21,961 --> 00:25:27,140
It's fine on a cut, on a piercing action, it can go clean through and push them apart.

322
00:25:28,360 --> 00:25:34,080
As the decaying Roman Empire became increasingly fragmented, so too did the fields of battle.

323
00:25:35,180 --> 00:25:40,200
The Imperial Legion scattered, trying to hold the far-flung territories together.

324
00:25:41,740 --> 00:25:44,800
And the scope of military engagements shrank.

325
00:25:46,900 --> 00:25:50,580
Armsmakers responded with weapons tailored for a more mobile soldier.

326
00:25:50,581 --> 00:25:58,644
There seems to be, at least according to the records and according to some of the myths that come around, a lot more of individual fighting, one-on-one type of

327
00:25:58,645 --> 00:25:59,345
fighting.

328
00:25:59,940 --> 00:26:08,240
Combat at closer quarters compelled Roman and Goth ironsmiths to produce fewer slashing-type swords and make more practical stabbing weapons.

329
00:26:11,140 --> 00:26:16,600
A lot of the work we think at this time was done more with the tip than it was with most of the blade.

330
00:26:16,601 --> 00:26:20,540
Remember, even a blade made for fighting is still a considerable investment.

331
00:26:20,580 --> 00:26:25,040
So if you can use this more like a spear and thrust it into your enemy quickly,

332
00:26:25,200 --> 00:26:28,740
you can save the blade and subsequent work and so on that had to be done to it.

333
00:26:28,960 --> 00:26:31,360
The effects of this going into a person are quite shocking.

334
00:26:31,700 --> 00:26:33,420
You see you've got the nerves and so on on the skin.

335
00:26:33,680 --> 00:26:35,520
Then you go into deep tissue or organs.

336
00:26:36,120 --> 00:26:37,720
You're going to cause internal bleeding.

337
00:26:37,960 --> 00:26:40,480
You're going to take bits of fabric in there with you.

338
00:26:40,720 --> 00:26:42,040
It's just going to be a ripe mess.

339
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:47,420
The best way to kill him is to get in here and push up,

340
00:26:47,860 --> 00:26:49,040
because then you're going to get all the organs.

341
00:26:49,041 --> 00:26:54,480
You can't get past the rib cage, only if you get a very slight angle you get it in there.

342
00:26:55,440 --> 00:27:00,160
But we know if you actually come up underneath you'll damage all his organs and we only know

343
00:27:00,161 --> 00:27:05,270
that you only need to go in that far to actually damage any of his organs.

344
00:27:05,271 --> 00:27:06,720
So the thrust upwards

345
00:27:06,721 --> 00:27:08,312
would be the killer thrust.

346
00:27:08,313 --> 00:27:12,320
Also the thrust down here, the main artery here, so you're trying to

347
00:27:12,321 --> 00:27:17,603
get in here and you're trying to get out there and under his arm just here.

348
00:27:17,604 --> 00:27:18,800
You get in there,

349
00:27:19,040 --> 00:27:21,780
an inch or so, there's a main archery and he's dead.

350
00:27:23,880 --> 00:27:26,500
As the Goths and Romans modified their weaponry,

351
00:27:27,100 --> 00:27:29,040
their uneasy alliance deteriorated.

352
00:27:30,320 --> 00:27:34,540
In 377, just one year after sealing their pact with Rome,

353
00:27:34,541 --> 00:27:36,820
the Goths suffered a devastating famine,

354
00:27:37,380 --> 00:27:39,040
and Rome let them starve.

355
00:27:41,640 --> 00:27:43,540
The Goths revolted,

356
00:27:44,860 --> 00:27:48,780
unleashing their fury against the imperial army.

357
00:27:49,040 --> 00:27:52,040
Years of bloody conflict rocked the weakening empire.

358
00:27:57,440 --> 00:28:01,040
So we are, fourth century, Goth versus Roman.

359
00:28:11,440 --> 00:28:15,340
Okay, and here we have the male, my vest, protecting me against the shot.

360
00:28:15,341 --> 00:28:18,340
I've moved in close, so the edge just moves out against me.

361
00:28:19,040 --> 00:28:25,840
By 395, the Goths were primed to deliver the death blow.

362
00:28:26,740 --> 00:28:31,120
Their new king, Alaric, marched his warriors toward the imperial capital.

363
00:28:33,400 --> 00:28:36,960
In 410, he and the Goths achieved the unthinkable.

364
00:28:40,240 --> 00:28:42,580
They swiped the jewel of the Roman Empire.

365
00:28:43,420 --> 00:28:48,180
They take the city of Rome, but they do it essentially by cutting off the supplies of Rome.

366
00:28:48,181 --> 00:28:51,120
And therefore it was quite easy to starve it into submission.

367
00:28:51,600 --> 00:28:55,700
And eventually Rome, they opened the gates and Rome is sacked for the first time.

368
00:29:00,100 --> 00:29:04,160
But occupying the city of Rome wasn't Alaric's ultimate goal.

369
00:29:04,800 --> 00:29:07,700
He wanted a Goth homeland somewhere within the empire.

370
00:29:09,580 --> 00:29:11,760
Ten years after his shocking victory,

371
00:29:12,540 --> 00:29:15,060
officials of the dying empire finally granted him

372
00:29:15,061 --> 00:29:18,360
the first barbarian kingdom in southern Gaul.

373
00:29:19,540 --> 00:29:23,880
It was there that they encountered another tribe of barbarians, the Franks.

374
00:29:25,400 --> 00:29:30,540
Its warriors were masters of a small deadly throwing axe called the Francisca.

375
00:29:31,380 --> 00:29:34,800
They were weapons that could be launched from afar, like a javelin,

376
00:29:34,801 --> 00:29:43,200
but with a lot more power, and a power that was used by the fact that the axe turned on its axis as it was being thrown.

377
00:29:43,201 --> 00:29:46,680
The first thing that this weapon is going to be able to do,

378
00:29:46,681 --> 00:29:48,760
it'll distract the opponent, at least.

379
00:29:49,640 --> 00:29:51,000
Take his eye off the fighting.

380
00:29:51,600 --> 00:29:54,900
If it gets through, even if it doesn't break the male shirt,

381
00:29:55,380 --> 00:30:00,420
it will injure him, it will stop him from continuing in the battle.

382
00:30:01,260 --> 00:30:04,320
If you're lucky, if you're good, hit him in the head,

383
00:30:04,980 --> 00:30:08,280
hit him in the neck, break his head in two, cut it clean off.

384
00:30:08,860 --> 00:30:11,760
Really powerful, really brutal weapon.

385
00:30:13,200 --> 00:30:16,520
Another strategic plus of the Francisco was its unpredictability.

386
00:30:17,640 --> 00:30:20,280
If you threw the axe to the ground at the proper angle,

387
00:30:21,060 --> 00:30:24,340
its lethal blade would ricochet wildly into the onrushing enemy.

388
00:30:25,100 --> 00:30:29,280
It describes a kind of erratic loop through the air,

389
00:30:29,440 --> 00:30:33,920
which when you're a defender, you're getting this kind of peculiar axe shape coming through.

390
00:30:34,800 --> 00:30:37,200
It's very hard to guess where it's going to hit you.

391
00:30:38,120 --> 00:30:41,020
Don't forget, this isn't just being thrown and then ignored.

392
00:30:41,021 --> 00:30:46,420
This is being followed by the angry man with it, who's now drawing another weapon to follow that axe.

393
00:30:47,440 --> 00:30:51,040
The Francisca had an S-shaped curve at the top of its iron head,

394
00:30:51,260 --> 00:30:54,000
and the lower edge of the blade was rounded inward.

395
00:30:55,360 --> 00:31:01,040
The center of the axe head formed an angle of approximately 90 to 115 degrees to the handle.

396
00:31:02,460 --> 00:31:06,140
Most Francisca axe heads weighed between a half pound and three pounds,

397
00:31:06,460 --> 00:31:08,880
and their length varied between five and 12 inches.

398
00:31:11,020 --> 00:31:14,300
Look at the shape of the head on this. Look how much weight there is.

399
00:31:14,580 --> 00:31:16,240
All at the head of a haft.

400
00:31:16,520 --> 00:31:18,800
What that means is it's very heavy up here.

401
00:31:19,200 --> 00:31:23,020
That way, even if you miss with the edge,

402
00:31:23,320 --> 00:31:26,700
you've still got a lot of power, a lot of weight there in a very small reach.

403
00:31:26,800 --> 00:31:29,340
So you can come in very, very fast indeed.

404
00:31:33,340 --> 00:31:34,820
Look at that.

405
00:31:36,160 --> 00:31:36,860
Straight through.

406
00:31:38,420 --> 00:31:39,560
Man's now without a shield.

407
00:31:39,561 --> 00:31:41,940
The next shot, that's him dead.

408
00:31:45,640 --> 00:31:51,780
Wielding the Francisca, the Franks sliced through every barbarian tribe that challenged them, including the Goths.

409
00:31:52,300 --> 00:31:55,400
By the 6th century AD, all of Gaul was theirs.

410
00:31:56,660 --> 00:32:01,380
The Franks renamed the region, inspired by the lethal Francisca that helped them seize it.

411
00:32:02,220 --> 00:32:05,200
They called it Francia. Today, it's France.

412
00:32:05,201 --> 00:32:13,900
across the english channel yet another barbarian tribe had stormed the shores of britain and they

413
00:32:13,901 --> 00:32:26,800
were making waves with their own battle technology after the collapse of the roman empire in ad 410

414
00:32:26,801 --> 00:32:32,260
its soldiers occupying britain pulled out their withdrawal created a power vacuum

415
00:32:32,261 --> 00:32:42,300
And as word spread across Europe, a number of barbarian tribes made the treacherous sea journey across the English Channel to claim their piece of the island.

416
00:32:44,940 --> 00:32:52,400
For years, the competing tribes battled for supremacy, until one, the Anglo-Saxons, emerged victorious.

417
00:32:53,620 --> 00:32:58,580
In the new British kingdom they established, their culture and religious beliefs prevailed.

418
00:32:58,581 --> 00:33:05,340
They believed in a large group of gods who all had power.

419
00:33:06,040 --> 00:33:12,600
And they also seemed to have believed that you needed to be buried with things to take with you to the afterlife.

420
00:33:13,420 --> 00:33:19,480
In 1939, archaeologists unearthed Anglo-Saxon burial mounds in the town of Sutton Hoo.

421
00:33:20,280 --> 00:33:24,240
One of the graves contained the remains of a warrior next to the remnants of a ship.

422
00:33:24,241 --> 00:33:29,240
The discovery shed vivid new light on the militaristic culture he inhabited.

423
00:33:31,040 --> 00:33:39,720
They saw not just a warrior of great renown, but a warrior of great renown whose entire accoutrement was still there.

424
00:33:39,721 --> 00:33:46,920
In fact, the rivets from the boat were still there, so they could actually see how large the boat was that he had been buried in.

425
00:33:47,840 --> 00:33:50,960
Sutton Hoo yielded many weapons containing Iron Age medals.

426
00:33:51,960 --> 00:33:54,200
Gold wire decorated the warrior's sword.

427
00:33:55,420 --> 00:33:58,580
Bronze panels coated with tin, lined his steel helmet.

428
00:33:59,460 --> 00:34:04,340
And an iron boss, used to protect his hand, capped his spectacular shield.

429
00:34:05,140 --> 00:34:07,800
All proof of his culture's mastery of metallurgy.

430
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:12,940
When the Anglo-Saxons weren't duking it out on the battlefield,

431
00:34:13,420 --> 00:34:15,980
they lived in small villages scattered across England.

432
00:34:16,940 --> 00:34:19,360
In the center of each village was the Mead Hall,

433
00:34:19,361 --> 00:34:25,440
an oblong structure where the lord of the tribe conducted business and tipped a few flagons.

434
00:34:27,060 --> 00:34:33,740
Members of a reenactment group called Regia Anglorum have resurrected this bygone institution of Anglo-Saxon culture.

435
00:34:34,660 --> 00:34:38,920
Behind me is a reconstruction of an Anglo-Saxon mead hall.

436
00:34:39,620 --> 00:34:43,500
An Anglo-Saxon mead hall is a place where the lord gathers his taxes,

437
00:34:43,501 --> 00:34:52,960
entertains guests and in defensive crisis gathers his troops either to attack or to defend

438
00:34:52,961 --> 00:34:58,580
mead was a honey-sweetened alcoholic beverage that the saxons drank to break the chill of the

439
00:34:58,581 --> 00:35:04,560
harsh english winters it filled every cup during their pre-war rituals the drinking in the mead

440
00:35:04,561 --> 00:35:09,140
hall the men drank together and swore pledges to each other that when the time came and the enemy

441
00:35:09,141 --> 00:35:12,000
came against them with spear and with shield, with point and edge,

442
00:35:12,340 --> 00:35:14,980
that they would be able to stand together in the shield war

443
00:35:14,981 --> 00:35:16,080
and fight together.

444
00:35:16,500 --> 00:35:18,500
The men that drank together fought together

445
00:35:18,501 --> 00:35:20,100
because they had said they would.

446
00:35:21,280 --> 00:35:23,680
After the drinking came the call to arms.

447
00:35:24,720 --> 00:35:29,200
On the walls, the weapons of the Lord and his warriors.

448
00:35:29,560 --> 00:35:34,200
We have the lightweight throwing javelin discharged at the enemy.

449
00:35:34,201 --> 00:35:43,600
The fighting spear, with its sharp edge and point, used overarm in conjunction with a shield.

450
00:35:44,900 --> 00:35:50,620
The fighting spear had been a battlefield mainstay in Britain since the Celts first thumbed their noses at the Romans.

451
00:35:51,700 --> 00:35:53,920
You could always count on it to make its point.

452
00:35:57,760 --> 00:36:02,180
Now to kill him from here, I'll probably look to get a spear into him as hard as I can.

453
00:36:02,181 --> 00:36:03,160
Just keep going at him.

454
00:36:03,161 --> 00:36:04,860
I put a shield down, protect my body.

455
00:36:05,420 --> 00:36:08,120
After all, he is a trained killer and can cause me all sorts of mischief.

456
00:36:08,460 --> 00:36:10,440
And then a few thrusts to his face.

457
00:36:10,800 --> 00:36:14,640
Perhaps even after that, quick smash with the shield as I move on to continue the fight.

458
00:36:16,000 --> 00:36:20,500
One difference between the Anglo-Saxon spears and earlier models was its spear point.

459
00:36:22,640 --> 00:36:26,220
Saxon metal workers crafted it to inflict deeper and deadlier wounds.

460
00:36:27,920 --> 00:36:30,920
If you look at this wonderfully made spear point,

461
00:36:30,921 --> 00:36:35,880
We recognize it's thinner at the point, it's even still sharp to a certain extent.

462
00:36:36,160 --> 00:36:40,820
This will penetrate the body and penetrate quite far into the body,

463
00:36:41,020 --> 00:36:45,040
to the point where it probably won't go through, but will get stuck there.

464
00:36:45,300 --> 00:36:49,220
The individual is probably having to pull with all his might to get it out of the body

465
00:36:49,221 --> 00:36:53,360
as the flesh comes around this and circles around the smaller socket.

466
00:36:54,900 --> 00:37:00,280
The most distinctive Saxon weapon was a single-edged knife called the sea axe.

467
00:37:00,920 --> 00:37:06,280
The CX, or knife, the weapon for which the Saxons were named.

468
00:37:06,840 --> 00:37:11,140
A utility weapon, a general purpose weapon, a weapon of war.

469
00:37:13,060 --> 00:37:16,840
The Anglo-Saxons repelled every invader that dared to challenge them.

470
00:37:20,280 --> 00:37:25,000
Eventually, their pagan culture fell, not at the point of an enemy's sword,

471
00:37:25,360 --> 00:37:28,600
but as Christianity transformed Britain starting in the 7th century.

472
00:37:28,601 --> 00:37:35,180
still their legacy endures in the form of the international language they left behind

473
00:37:35,181 --> 00:37:40,540
they were pretty good at what they did as far as weapons were concerned here we all are we

474
00:37:40,541 --> 00:37:44,700
speak english what better indication do you need of the fact that they were successful

475
00:37:44,701 --> 00:37:55,380
for nearly 1500 years the barbarian cultures had slashed their way into history

476
00:37:55,381 --> 00:38:02,380
Battle after battle, they proved their weaponry was as effective as that of any foe.

477
00:38:02,381 --> 00:38:08,380
But what was the best weapon of the barbarian age?

478
00:38:08,381 --> 00:38:15,380
Surprisingly, the creators of a video game may have the answer.

479
00:38:15,381 --> 00:38:23,980
Imagine you're a barbarian.

480
00:38:24,440 --> 00:38:26,580
That'll be easier for some compared to others.

481
00:38:28,100 --> 00:38:30,960
What barbarian weapon would you carry into battle?

482
00:38:31,780 --> 00:38:33,000
Which was deadliest?

483
00:38:35,240 --> 00:38:36,780
Historians can only speculate.

484
00:38:38,620 --> 00:38:41,760
But the game designers at the Creative Assembly in England

485
00:38:41,761 --> 00:38:45,680
have devised a formula that rates the Iron Age's most lethal weapons

486
00:38:45,681 --> 00:38:48,120
against the defenses used to counter them.

487
00:38:50,620 --> 00:38:53,180
The results play out as bloody good fun

488
00:38:53,181 --> 00:38:56,300
in the animated game Barbarian Invasion.

489
00:39:01,040 --> 00:39:02,960
We created a huge spreadsheet.

490
00:39:04,720 --> 00:39:09,060
And with that we actually kind of started to rank those weapons and shields

491
00:39:09,061 --> 00:39:11,000
compared to each other in the armor that they used.

492
00:39:11,001 --> 00:39:16,560
And then also, on top of that, we layered the actual skill of the people who were using those weapons and armor.

493
00:39:17,360 --> 00:39:23,680
And the result of that is that we ended up with a very, very complex network of connections.

494
00:39:24,820 --> 00:39:30,820
For each weapon, designers calculated an attack value based on its capacity to penetrate defenses.

495
00:39:31,820 --> 00:39:37,660
The very high attack rating means that it's more likely, when it strikes somebody,

496
00:39:37,661 --> 00:39:40,420
to knock them over and certainly to kill them

497
00:39:40,421 --> 00:39:42,660
than any other type of sword.

498
00:39:42,661 --> 00:39:45,820
Take swords, for example.

499
00:39:45,821 --> 00:39:48,220
Designers rated the Roman gladius highly

500
00:39:48,221 --> 00:39:51,300
because it could cut effectively into armor.

501
00:39:51,301 --> 00:39:54,160
But they ranked the slashing Celtic sword even higher

502
00:39:54,161 --> 00:39:55,980
because of its added length.

503
00:39:55,981 --> 00:39:58,200
Celtic sword could inflict terrible damage.

504
00:39:58,201 --> 00:40:02,020
Swung by a strong man, it would probably

505
00:40:02,021 --> 00:40:06,220
take your head clean off, or it would split your head open.

506
00:40:06,221 --> 00:40:08,680
While no other sword ranked higher than that of the Celts,

507
00:40:09,820 --> 00:40:13,940
the Hun bow emerged as one of the game's deadliest overall weapons.

508
00:40:14,540 --> 00:40:16,360
It would be much more likely to kill a man,

509
00:40:16,700 --> 00:40:21,300
and therefore more likely as well to knock the person over,

510
00:40:21,620 --> 00:40:25,640
because obviously the strength of the bow, the killing power of the bow,

511
00:40:26,000 --> 00:40:29,160
relates to its velocity and its penetrative power.

512
00:40:29,660 --> 00:40:33,640
A Hunnish bow would probably punch straight through most Roman armor.

513
00:40:34,440 --> 00:40:36,080
It's a very efficient weapon.

514
00:40:36,220 --> 00:40:38,020
It's got a good pull on it.

515
00:40:38,021 --> 00:40:40,200
It's got the kind of pull that you would expect

516
00:40:40,201 --> 00:40:43,760
from a modern sports bow.

517
00:40:43,761 --> 00:40:46,140
Score one for Attila's game.

518
00:40:46,141 --> 00:40:48,900
But the Hunbow lost points for a surprising reason.

519
00:40:51,460 --> 00:40:54,580
Its diminished performance in the rain.

520
00:40:54,581 --> 00:40:56,700
The Hunbow was generally more vulnerable in the rain

521
00:40:56,701 --> 00:40:58,800
because it was made of composite materials.

522
00:40:58,801 --> 00:41:03,680
And so it wasn't as sturdy as a Western style bow,

523
00:41:03,681 --> 00:41:05,180
which was a single piece of wood.

524
00:41:06,220 --> 00:41:08,120
It's made of several layers of material.

525
00:41:08,340 --> 00:41:10,280
When it gets damp, the glue starts giving way

526
00:41:10,281 --> 00:41:11,620
that's holding the different layers together,

527
00:41:11,700 --> 00:41:13,060
and it actually starts delaminating.

528
00:41:15,740 --> 00:41:18,200
On a rainy day, trash your hon bow

529
00:41:18,201 --> 00:41:20,400
and grab what designers deemed was perhaps

530
00:41:20,401 --> 00:41:23,340
the deadliest weapon of all, despite its small size,

531
00:41:23,720 --> 00:41:25,280
the Francisca throwing axe.

532
00:41:27,020 --> 00:41:29,100
With the Francisca, when it hits a man,

533
00:41:29,101 --> 00:41:34,920
we actually decided that it should have two types of value.

534
00:41:36,220 --> 00:41:41,420
bashing value, and penetration value.

535
00:41:43,420 --> 00:41:45,740
I can actually throw my weapon at the enemy,

536
00:41:46,260 --> 00:41:47,940
and that's going to cause them some harm.

537
00:41:48,640 --> 00:41:50,200
Then when it comes into close combat,

538
00:41:50,400 --> 00:41:52,860
I've also got the ability to block his blows.

539
00:41:53,320 --> 00:41:57,080
I've got the ability to fight hand-to-hand with somebody with a sword,

540
00:41:57,420 --> 00:42:00,580
and at the same time, I can cause some pretty lethal wounds.

541
00:42:00,581 --> 00:42:08,820
We've saddled the barbarians with a name that disparages their cultures and their contributions.

542
00:42:11,820 --> 00:42:15,340
But there's no denying the inventiveness of their weapons designers.

543
00:42:18,440 --> 00:42:24,040
Their ingenious arsenals powered the march of armies across Europe for century after century,

544
00:42:24,540 --> 00:42:27,320
in battles that helped determine the course of history.

545
00:42:27,321 --> 00:42:34,320
Not bad for a bunch of barbarians.

